% TRIO, first part, bars 153-184 (E major, un poco meno mosso).
% c035: 169 Fl 1 prints "1." (no label was reprinted after the shared rest).
% 153-168: the trio tune T in Cl 1, dolce, over the harp's off-beat chords and a soft E-B drone in Hn 3-4 (a musette).
%   T is new material: it rises by step and a fourth (G sharp-A-B-E) and falls back through D sharp, the leading tone (cyclic-notes:
%   the scherzo prepares D sharp as a spelling for the finale's violas). Cadences: half 160, authentic 167.
% 169-184: T in Vln I, an octave higher; Fl 1 doubles it heterophonically (the same line with passing notes and turns: still unused in I and II);
%   the cellos have a countermelody of their own (a melodic bass line), the double basses pizzicato.

% c036: Fl 1 p, not pp: its line climbs to A6, the flute's top fourth, where soft dynamics and intonation are hardest to control (Adler PDF 174, 186)
% c051: 169-176 Fl 1 rests: its heterophony sat at the violins' pitch and shadowed T note for note (Sebesky PDF 211-212, Ex. 279-280: a background that
%   shadows the solo in near-parallel motion distorts the focus); it joins at 177 for the climb to A6, the added colour saved for the climax (PDF 209).
% c051: 172, 176 (and 212, 216 in tC) the cellos' countermelody moves on beat 3 where T holds, carrying the phrase into the next (Sebesky PDF 207-208, Ex. 273).
% c061: 166, 182 (and 222 in tC, 355 in the coda) T's cadence takes a real V4-3 suspension: the E of the cadential 6/4 is tied over the barline
%   and resolves to D sharp on beat 2 (was D sharp minim on the downbeat). Aldwell-Schachter Unit 21 PDF 352 sec. 8 (the cadential suspension
%   emphasizes the leading tone), PDF 346 sec. 1 (a suspension animates and individualizes a line through rhythm), PDF 348 (dissonant suspensions
%   take an accent, their resolutions are softer). No other part has D sharp on the downbeat (harp and violas enter on beat 2: PDF 352 sec. 12);
%   182 Fl 1 E6 with the violins on beat 1, its turn moved to beat 2 (it had D sharp 6 against the violins' E6).
% c093: 180-184, the second statement's consequent modulates to B (V): ii6/5 of B over E (180), cadential 6/4 and V7 over F sharp (181-182), B major (183): T's own cadence D sharp-F sharp-B~ | B-A sharp-C sharp | B transposed to B (a fourth lower in pitch), with its 4-3 suspension.
%   The trio's two A phrases no longer cadence in the same place, and the middle part's C sharp minor (185) enters as ii of B / vi of E (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 14,
%   PDF 222 Ex. 14-10 modulating consequent; PDF 224 quatrain: first and second A usually cadence in different places; PDF 212 Heidenroeslein: the repeat begins alike, then turns to V).
% c093: 175 (and 215 in tC) the cellos hold C sharp 3 instead of A sharp 2-B2: the V7/V's raised 4th (A sharp) is in the violas alone, not doubled, and no cello B sounds against it (A-S PDF 214).
% c098: 165-166, 181-182 (and 221-222 in tC, 355 in the coda) the cadential 4-3 is shaded: < on the preparation into the tied dissonance, > from it to the resolution (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 348: dissonant suspensions receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer; PDF 350 sec. 3: the resolution is a relaxation, a decrease in intensity).
tAFluteOne = { R2.*16 | R2.*8 | R2.*8 | }
% c121: T bar 6 (158, 174, 214; coda 346 in A) cis4 fis2 (was cis2 fis4): the second phrase's peak F sharp lands on beat 2 and is held, so phrase 2 is no longer a rhythmic copy of phrase 1 a step higher (open since c001); the long note off the downbeat is the device of Adler's lyrical string examples (PDF 74 Ex. III-5 Tchaikovsky 5/ii, PDF 75 Ex. III-7 Brahms 3/iii) and a hint of the movement's hemiola.
tAClOne = { gis'2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" a'4 | b'2 e''4 | dis''4. cis''8 b'4 | a'4 gis'2) | a'2( b'4 | cis''4\< fis''2-\tweak stencil ##f \p\> | e''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \p dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) \breathe | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4 a''4~ | a''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 a'4) | gis'4-\tweak stencil ##f \p( b'4 e''4\<~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \p fis''4 | e''2.~\> | e''2) r4\! | R2.*16 | }  % c176: breath at the half-way cadence of T (bar 8 of the tune), where the horn drones also rest; 16 bars of slurred melody otherwise (Sebesky PDF 24: a slur is played in one breath; PDF 70: give the player breathing spaces)
tAHnThree = { b2.~\pp | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | R2.*16 | }
tAHnFour = { e2.~\pp | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2 r4 | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2 r4 | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2 r4 | e2.~ | e4 r4 r4 | e2.~ | e2 r4 | R2.*16 | }
% c151: 166 Hn 4 leaves the drone E3 on beat 2, as T's cadential 4-3 (Cl 1 E5-D sharp 5) resolves, and takes it up again on the tonic at 167.
%   After the pizz. B1 dies, the held E3 was the lowest sustained sound under the V7, so the suspended E kept sounding through the resolution
%   (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 348: the resolution a relaxation; PDF 352: an unclear sound weakens the resolution; PDF 368: over a pedal, dissonances among
%   the upper parts resolve normally). The other cadences of T (182, 222, 355) already resolve with no E left sounding.
tATimp = { e,4\pp r4 r4 | R2.*3 | R2.*11 | e,4\pp r4 r4 | R2.*16 | }  % c055: pp restated at 168 after 14 bars rest (Adler II PDF 299, item 4); "soft sticks" moved to 146
% c076: "non arpegg." at 153: the off-beat chords are struck, not rolled (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46: harp chords are broken unless marked otherwise; rolled chords in this score carry the arpeggio sign).
tAHarpR = { r4 <gis b e'>4^\markup \small \italic "non arpegg." <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <a b dis'>4 <a b dis'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <e a cis'>4 <e a cis'>4 | r4 <a cis' fis'>4 <a cis' fis'>4 | r4 <cis' e' fis'>4 <cis' e' fis'>4 | r4 <b dis' fis'>4 <b dis' fis'>4 | r4 <gis cis' e'>4 <gis cis' e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <a cis' fis'>4 <a cis' fis'>4 | r4 <a cis' fis'>4 <a cis' fis'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <a b dis'>4 <a b dis'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c094: 169-184 the harp's left hand rests: the violins' statement of T is for the strings alone (c074), and the harp, which had played every bar from 153 to 192, returns fresh at 185
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 53: plucked strings soon become wearisome and should be used at reasonable intervals). The basses' pizzicato keeps the bass.
% c099: harp pp (was p) under Cl 1's p dolce, one degree below the solo, as the coda gives it under Hn 1 at 341 (Casanelles PDF 271-272: a dynamic marks the loudness that results, not the player's effort; Mahler marks the accompanying line softer than the line in front).
tAHarpL = { e,4\pp r4 r4  | e,4 r4 r4  | b,,4 r4 r4  | e,4 r4 r4  | a,,4 r4 r4  | fis,,4 r4 r4  | fis,,4 r4 r4  | b,,4 r4 r4  | cis,4 r4 r4  | gis,,4 r4 r4  | fis,,4 r4 r4  | a,,4 r4 r4  | b,,4 r4 r4  | b,,4 r4 r4  | e,4 r4 r4  | e,4 r4 r4  | R2.*16 | }
% c076: "nat." moves from 173 to the E6 at 170 beat 3: sul A stays within the fourth position (G sharp 5-A5-B5, 169-170), Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 26: a high note on a lower string is limited to the octave or ninth of the open string (A5/B5 on the A string); E6-D sharp 6-C sharp 6 at 170-171 were in the seventh position.
tAVlnOne = { R2.*16 | gis''2(\mp^\markup \italic "espress." a''4 | b''2 e'''4) | dis'''4.( cis'''8 b''4) | a''4( gis''2) | a''2( b''4 | cis'''4\< fis'''2-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\>) | e'''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \mp dis'''8 cis'''4 | ais''4 b''2) | cis'''2(\< dis'''4 | e'''4 gis'''4) a'''4~( | a'''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \mf\> gis'''8 fis'''4 | cis'''4 b''4 gis''4) | dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp( fis''4 b''4\<~ | b''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf\> ais''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp cis'''4) | b''2.~\> | b''2 r4\! | }  % c121: no sul A / nat. at 169-170 (c031, c076 reversed): after c076 the marking covered only G sharp 5-A5-B5 and changed string on the phrase's top note E6 inside the slur; on one string the phrase keeps one colour (Adler PDF 73: in expressive passages all tones of equal texture; PDF 75: Brahms 3/iii sul A stays within the ninth, B flat 5)
tAVlnTwo = { R2.*16 | gis'2(\mp^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4) | dis''4.( cis''8 b'4) | a'4( gis'2) | a'2( b'4 | cis''4\< fis''2-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\>) | e''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \mp dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4) a''4~( | a''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \mf\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 gis'4) | dis'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp( fis'4 b'4\<~ | b'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf\> ais'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp cis''4) | b'2.~\> | b'2 r4\! | }  % c011: T an octave under Vln I (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 58-59, 62: a melody above the middle of the 5th octave is doubled an octave below)
% c089: 172-173 violas E4-D4 on beat 3 of the tune's fourth bar, then C sharp 4: a passing seventh, I - V7/IV - IV, so the second and third statements of the
%   trio tune are not harmonised exactly like the first (153-156 stays plain). A-S PDF 418-419 (Ex. 25-1: applied and passing applied chords in a plain theme), PDF 399 (the 7th resolves
%   down by step). The temporary leading tone G sharp is in the tune only on beat 3 (cellos have moved to F sharp, PDF 419: not doubled); F sharp (9th) -> E and D -> C sharp in 173/213.
tAVla = { R2.*16 | r4 <b e'>4--(\pp^\markup \italic "div." <b e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <a dis'>4--( <a dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b d'>4--) | r4 <a cis'>4--( <a cis'>4--) | r4 <a fis'>4--( <a fis'>4--) | r4 <ais e'>4--( <ais e'>4--) | r4 <b dis'>4--( <b dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <cis' e'>4--( <cis' e'>4--) | r4 <gis cis'>4--( <gis cis'>4--) | r4 <b dis'>4--( <b dis'>4--) | r4 <fis cis'>4--( <fis cis'>4--) | r4 <fis dis'>4--( <fis dis'>4--) | r4 <fis dis'>4--( <fis dis'>4--) | }  % c011: violas div. take Vln II's off-beats, below the tune
% c037: 153-168 the cellos rest (they doubled the harp's left hand and the basses' pizzicato note for note), so their arco countermelody at 169
%   enters fresh and the musette under Cl 1 is lighter (Adler II PDF 228: Stravinsky lightens the texture so the next entrance is fresh;
%   PDF 205: the bass is the basses' pizzicato, the cello part is independent).
% c158: 177-180 the cello countermelody swells with the tune's climb (hidden mp/p targets, c138) and tapers at 183 with it, one level under, as at 209-224 since c157 (Sebesky PDF 205, 207, Ex. 269: the counter-line has its own arc in step with the lead's).
% c192: 181 the cellos hold F sharp 3 (was B2-D sharp 3), so the cadential 6/4 of B (c093) stands over a stationary bass and the ii6/5 of 180 leads to V, not to a root-position B tonic
%   (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 152 sec. 1: the 6/4 embellishes V, it is not I; PDF 153 sec. 3 and PDF 160 pt. 2: the 4th resolves down by step and is not taken away by leap; PDF 154 sec. 5-6: double the bass, the 6/4 resolves over a stationary bass; PDF 148 rule 3: intermediate harmonies do not lead to I).
tAVc = { R2.*16 | b,4(\p^\markup \italic "arco" e2 | gis2.) | fis2( a4 | gis2 fis4) | e4( cis2~ | cis4 a,2) | cis2.( | dis2 fis4) | e4(\< gis2~ | gis4 e2) | a2(-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> gis4 | e2.-\tweak stencil ##f \p) | fis2.( | cis2 e4) | dis2.~\> | dis2 r4\! | }
% c148: 153 basses pp (was p): the pizzicato low E/B on every downbeat sat at the solo's level under Cl 1's p dolce, with harp, horns and timpani pp (c099; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 50: low notes and metrical downbeats already come across strongly). They return to p at 169 under the violins' mp.
tACb = { e,4\pp r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4\p r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | }
% c074: 177-184 Fl 1 rests: the violins' statement of T is the strings' own (Vln I, Vln II, div. violas, cellos, basses), and the flute's doubling at A6
%   is heard first at 217, where c069 reserved it (Adler I PDF 143: a theme is first scored for the strings before it is coloured or doubled by others;
%   PDF 129 and 135: the higher colour is saved for the more important later statement). Fl 1's first trio entry is now the forte call at 197.
% c081: T bars 10-11 (162, 178, 218, 350): the climax A is anticipated on beat 3 of bar 10 and tied over the bar line (was e4 gis2 | a4. gis8 fis4, now e4 gis4 a4~ | a4. gis8 fis4).
%   The phrase's highest note becomes its longest and a syncopation, the only bar-line tie before the cadence (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 3 PDF 50: long and high notes take accent;
%   PDF 51: a note begun on a weak beat and held through the strong beat contradicts the metre; PDF 56, Ex. 3-19: the anticipation held over into the consonance, here I6 -> ii).
%   Dvorak 9/iii's E-major tune (Ob 1, 68-76) ties over five bar lines in nine bars. Theme change (third phrase only); violins re-bow on the A.
% c112: 157 harp RH E3-A3-C#4 (voicing only; removes the 5ths Cl 1 B4-C#5 / harp E4-F#4 at 157-158). Plain harmony unchanged.
